[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Insta360 Mic Pro charging case with two transmitters and a receiver.
AUDIO: 'Over the next few minutes, I’ll show&nbsp; you everything you need to know to use&nbsp;&nbsp; the Insta360 Mic Pro properly. I’ll show you its&nbsp; hidden features and how to get the most out of the&nbsp;&nbsp; Mic Pro. To make this tutorial easier to follow,&nbsp; it’s divided into 13 chapters that correspond to&nbsp;&nbsp; the Mic Pro’s most important features. If you’d&nbsp; like to rewatch or rewind to any part of this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guide at any time, I’ve included timestamps below&nbsp; to make it easy. And if you want to find out more&nbsp;&nbsp; detailed information about the Insta360 Luna&nbsp; Ultra or buy it, you’ll also find links to do&nbsp;&nbsp; so in the video description. And many thanks to Insta360&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for sponsoring this video I have the bundle with the&nbsp;&nbsp; charging case and the two transmitters here.&nbsp; There are other bundles available, but the&nbsp;&nbsp; basic functionality of the microphones remains the&nbsp; same. This bundle includes two transmitters—which&nbsp;&nbsp; are essentially the microphones themselves—a&nbsp; receiver that you can connect to a camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smartphone, or computer, a USB-C adapter for the&nbsp; receiver, an audio cable that lets you connect&nbsp;&nbsp; the receiver to a camera’s microphone input, for&nbsp; example, two windscreens that you can attach to&nbsp;&nbsp; the transmitters to reduce wind noise, two back&nbsp; clips and two magnets to attach the transmitters&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:54] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of the back of an Insta360 Mic Pro transmitter, showing the Insta360 logo and magnetic attachment points.
AUDIO: 'to your clothing, the charging case, which&nbsp; is used to store and charge the microphones,&nbsp;&nbsp; a charging cable, and a carry pouch to safely&nbsp; store everything. So, quite a lot. I’ll now&nbsp;&nbsp; show you step by step how to use each part. And we’ll start with the most important part—the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transmitter—and how to attach it properly. There are two ways to attach the transmitter&nbsp;&nbsp; to your clothing. These two magnets, which you&nbsp; simply place under your clothing and then attach&nbsp;&nbsp; the microphone to. And the back clips, which you&nbsp; can use to clip the microphone to your clothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The back clips are not magnetic, so you’ll need&nbsp; to attach them to the microphone first. They have&nbsp;&nbsp; two small guides on both outer sides. At the same&nbsp; time, the microphone has two small slots on the&nbsp;&nbsp; back. Place the clip onto the microphone so that&nbsp; the guides fit into these slots. To do this, make&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sure the side of the clip with the label faces&nbsp; backward and the bottom of the clip faces the&nbsp;&nbsp; pins. Now slide the clip upward until it clicks&nbsp; into place. To remove it, simply slide it downward&nbsp;&nbsp; in the opposite direction. It takes a little&nbsp; force at first—but it gets easier with time.&nbsp;'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Normally, you position the microphone so that&nbsp; the microphone opening faces the sound source&nbsp;&nbsp; and is about a hand’s width away from&nbsp; your mouth. However, since the Mic Pro&nbsp;&nbsp; actually has three microphones and microphone&nbsp; inputs, the direction isn’t that critical.&nbsp;'

[02:08] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of an Insta360 Mic Pro transmitter with a furry windscreen attached.
AUDIO: 'The back of the transmitter is magnetic—this&nbsp; allows you to attach the transmitter to metal&nbsp;&nbsp; objects even without the clip. This can be very&nbsp; handy. If you’re recording outdoors and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp; even slightly windy, you should definitely use the&nbsp; windscreen. It significantly reduces wind noise.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'To attach it, slide it onto the transmitter from&nbsp; the top and make sure it snaps into place on the&nbsp;&nbsp; sides. But be aware that the windscreen included&nbsp; in the bundle only protects the top microphone.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If it’s very windy, you’ll need to adjust the&nbsp; microphone so that it records primarily through&nbsp;&nbsp; this microphone—for example, using the cardioid&nbsp; setting. You’ll see how to do that later. If&nbsp;&nbsp; needed, there’s also a windscreen accessory&nbsp; available that protects the side microphones&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as well. By the way, you’ll find links to&nbsp; all accessories in the video description.&nbsp; The transmitter has a range of up to&nbsp; 400 meters to the receiver. However,&nbsp;&nbsp; this range applies only when connected to the&nbsp; receiver and under optimal conditions. When&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connected directly to a camera, such as the&nbsp; Luna Ultra, the maximum range is 50 meters.&nbsp; Okay—now we’ve attached the transmitter properly.&nbsp; Let’s take a closer look at how to operate it.&nbsp; The transmitter has a USB-C port on the bottom,&nbsp; two buttons, and an LED light on each side.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:16] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of an Insta360 Mic Pro transmitter, showing the side with a blue LED light and a speaker grill.
AUDIO: 'You can use the USB-C port to charge the&nbsp; transmitter if you don’t have a charging case,&nbsp;&nbsp; download internal recordings, or—and this is also&nbsp; interesting—connect a lavalier microphone and use&nbsp;&nbsp; the transmitter solely as a transmission device. The right LED light is the internal recording&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light, so it indicates whether the microphone&nbsp; is currently making an internal recording—I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp; explain what an internal recording is in&nbsp; a bit more detail shortly. The left light,&nbsp;&nbsp; on the other hand, is the status and connection&nbsp; light. It shows whether a device is connected,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whether a feature like noise cancellation is&nbsp; currently active, or—while charging—what the&nbsp;&nbsp; battery level is. Right now, it’s flashing blue&nbsp; slowly, which means no device is connected. But&nbsp;&nbsp; it also means the microphone is turned on—which&nbsp; happens automatically when you take it out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'charging case. The button on the left is the power&nbsp; button. If you press and hold it for 2 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp; you’ll turn the microphone off. If you&nbsp; press and hold it for another 2 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp; you’ll turn the microphone back on. If you&nbsp; purchased the microphone without the charging&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:16] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A hand plugging a USB-C cable into the Insta360 Mic Pro transmitter, which displays a pixelated emoji on its E-Ink screen.
AUDIO: 'case, you’ll need to charge it via the USB-C&nbsp; port and then turn it on using the button.&nbsp; One very useful feature of the transmitter is&nbsp; that it has internal storage. Each transmitter&nbsp;&nbsp; has approximately 32 gigabytes. This means&nbsp; you can record an audio file using just the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transmitter—even without an additional&nbsp; camera or receiver. It’s very simple:&nbsp;&nbsp; as soon as the transmitter is turned on, just&nbsp; press the internal recording button briefly,&nbsp;&nbsp; and internal recording will start. You’ll&nbsp; know it’s recording when the red recording&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light stays on. Press the button again to stop&nbsp; the recording. You can connect the transmitter&nbsp;&nbsp; directly to your computer via a cable; the&nbsp; transmitter will be recognized as a drive,&nbsp;&nbsp; and you can copy the audio files. This is great&nbsp; for podcasts, for example, or if you simply need&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an audio recording without video. However, you&nbsp; can also make an internal recording simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp; while connected to a camera, effectively&nbsp; creating a backup copy of the audio recording. The power button has another function.&nbsp; If you press it twice quickly, you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'activate noise cancellation. The status&nbsp; LED will now flash or light up green,&nbsp;&nbsp; indicating that noise cancellation is&nbsp; active. Distracting background noise&nbsp;&nbsp; is reduced. We’ll see later that there&nbsp; are two types of noise cancellation.'

[05:23] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A hand holding the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver and another hand holding a transmitter with a pixelated emoji on its E-Ink screen.
AUDIO: 'In most cases, you won’t just want to record&nbsp; internally with the transmitter—you’ll want to&nbsp;&nbsp; connect it to a camera. You use the power button&nbsp; for this as well—if you press it three times&nbsp;&nbsp; quickly, the transmitter enters pairing mode.&nbsp; The status light will now flash blue rapidly.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Basically, there are two possible connection&nbsp; methods. You can connect the transmitter directly&nbsp;&nbsp; to a camera via Bluetooth. This works, of course,&nbsp; with devices from Insta360—such as the Luna Ultra,&nbsp;&nbsp; the X5, or the GO Ultra—but also with phones&nbsp; or computers. The second option is to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it to the receiver, which you then connect to the&nbsp; camera, your phone, or your computer. If, like me,&nbsp;&nbsp; you have the mic pro with the receiver and the&nbsp; charging case, and the receiver is turned on when&nbsp;&nbsp; you take it out of the case, the transmitter will&nbsp; automatically connect to the receiver. The status&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light on the transmitter will now light up solid&nbsp; blue. A solid status light indicates an active&nbsp;&nbsp; connection. You can now also see the audio level&nbsp; and status information for the microphone on the&nbsp;&nbsp; receiver. In my case, the receiver connected&nbsp; automatically. If that isn’t the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:24] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing the "Accessories" menu with options for "Bluetooth Accessories" and "Other Accessories."
AUDIO: 'place the receiver and transmitter in the&nbsp; charging case, and you’ll be asked if you want&nbsp;&nbsp; to pair them. If you don\'t have a charging case,&nbsp; activate the pairing mode on the transmitter,&nbsp;&nbsp; open the receiver\'s Control Center, select&nbsp; “Connection,” and then select “Pair.”&nbsp;'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you want to connect the transmitter directly&nbsp; to a camera via Bluetooth, press the power button&nbsp;&nbsp; three times briefly to re-enter pairing mode.&nbsp; You can now, for example, go to the settings&nbsp;&nbsp; on the Luna Ultra, tap one of the two microphone&nbsp; fields under “Wireless Mic,” and the microphone&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will connect without any problems. On the GO&nbsp; Ultra connect the microphone under “Accessories”&nbsp;&nbsp; and “Add Bluetooth Accessories.” On the X5,&nbsp; you\'ll find the connection under “Bluetooth”&nbsp;&nbsp; and “Bluetooth Microphone.” Always make sure the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'microphone is in pairing mode. The status LED will now light up&nbsp;&nbsp; solid blue again. So, to recap: slow flashing&nbsp; means the microphone is on but not connected;&nbsp;&nbsp; fast flashing means the microphone is in pairing&nbsp; mode. Solid light—the microphone is connected.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Green means noise cancellation is enabled. The&nbsp; microphone and your camera are now paired. In&nbsp;&nbsp; the future, both devices will recognize each&nbsp; other and connect automatically as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp; they’re both turned on. And that’s not all—if&nbsp; you’ve enabled Bluetooth Wake-Up on the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:36] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing settings for "Stereo Internal Recording," "Record with Internal Audio," and "Loop Recording."
AUDIO: 'the camera will turn on automatically as soon as&nbsp; you turn on the microphone. If you want to connect&nbsp;&nbsp; to a different camera later, simply switch back to&nbsp; pairing mode or place the microphone and receiver&nbsp;&nbsp; in the charging case to re-pair with the receiver. Once you’ve connected the Mic Pro to a camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can adjust many important microphone settings&nbsp; here in the menu, such as the pickup mode,&nbsp;&nbsp; auto gain, or the voice tone preset. I’ll explain&nbsp; these features in more detail in a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp; but as you can see here, you don’t&nbsp; actually need the receiver to do this.&nbsp;'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And there’s another extremely useful feature&nbsp; you should know about. Once the camera is&nbsp;&nbsp; connected to the transmitter, you can start&nbsp; and stop recording remotely by pressing the&nbsp;&nbsp; power button once. This can be quite handy. You can also connect the Luna Ultra to both&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transmitters via Bluetooth and record&nbsp; with both transmitters simultaneously.&nbsp; You can also connect the transmitter to a&nbsp; smartphone or computer without the receiver,&nbsp;&nbsp; using only Bluetooth. To do this, simply switch&nbsp; to pairing mode and search for the microphone in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:34] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A hand holding an iPhone with the home screen visible, highlighting the Insta360 app icon.
AUDIO: 'your device’s Bluetooth settings. My iPhone and my&nbsp; Mac both recognize the mic pro transmitter without&nbsp;&nbsp; any issues. But be warned: I wouldn’t recommend&nbsp; this, because the audio quality is very poor with&nbsp;&nbsp; this type of connection. Only use the mic pro&nbsp; without the receiver with supported Insta360&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'devices. For better audio quality when connecting&nbsp; to a smartphone or PC, you’ll need the receiver.&nbsp;&nbsp; This also applies whenever you want to connect&nbsp; the mic pro to a larger camera, like my Sony here. But before we take a look at the receiver, let’s&nbsp; check out one of the coolest features of the Mic&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pro: the E-Ink display and how to change the&nbsp; image. And it’s surprisingly easy. To do this,&nbsp;&nbsp; you’ll need the Insta360 app. You can find it&nbsp; in the app stores. Once you’ve opened the app,&nbsp;&nbsp; put the microphone back into pairing mode and&nbsp; tap the camera icon at the bottom of the app.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now you can connect the microphone. As you can&nbsp; see, you can also change all the microphone’s&nbsp;&nbsp; settings here, format the internal storage if&nbsp; needed, and even perform a firmware update.&nbsp;&nbsp; I’ll show you the best and most effective&nbsp; way to do this at the end of the video. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let’s change the background image. To do&nbsp; this, select “Custom Wallpaper.” A menu will&nbsp;&nbsp; open featuring a variety of wallpapers already&nbsp; provided by Insta360. There are emojis, pets,&nbsp;&nbsp; or even just letters or numbers. But what’s&nbsp; really cool is that you can upload your own&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image or logo. To do this, tap the plus sign&nbsp; and you can either enter your own text as a&nbsp;&nbsp; wallpaper or—much more interesting—select an&nbsp; image from your phone’s album. You can then&nbsp;&nbsp; adjust the position. Now you have the choice&nbsp; between “Natural” and “Solid.” “Natural” is&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'suitable for photos and more complex images,&nbsp; while “Solid” is best for simple logos. Under&nbsp;&nbsp; “Natural,” you can also adjust the brightness. There’s one thing you should know, though:&nbsp;&nbsp; the E-Ink display has the big advantage that&nbsp; it only uses power when changing the image—not&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'during normal operation. The downside, however,&nbsp; is that complex photos don’t display well,&nbsp;&nbsp; and colors aren’t always rendered&nbsp; accurately. So you’ll need to try out&nbsp;&nbsp; a few different logos to figure out what works&nbsp; best for you. But it’s a really cool feature.'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And there’s another feature that sets the Mic Pro&nbsp; apart: the directional pickup mode. You can set it&nbsp;&nbsp; right here on the camera itself, in the app—and,&nbsp; of course, you’ll also find this feature on the&nbsp;&nbsp; receiver in the transmitter settings. We’ll take&nbsp; a look at all the receiver settings later, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:52] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: The presenter holding an Insta360 Mic Pro transmitter with a windscreen, demonstrating its use.
AUDIO: 'wanted to cover this feature first because it’s&nbsp; particularly important. As we’ve already seen,&nbsp;&nbsp; the Mic Pro has not just one microphone, but&nbsp; three. Now it’s a matter of determining which&nbsp;&nbsp; direction the sound is primarily picked up from.&nbsp; Omnidirectional is the default mode. Here, sound&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is picked up from all directions, resulting in a&nbsp; very spacious sound. This is very well suited for&nbsp;&nbsp; vlogs. With Voice Focus or Super Directional, the&nbsp; goal is to primarily capture the voice directly in&nbsp;&nbsp; front of the microphone. Background noise from&nbsp; the sides or rear is suppressed, but distant&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background sounds are retained. As you can see,&nbsp; this feature is only available on the receiver,&nbsp;&nbsp; but not when connected to the camera. This is&nbsp; well-suited for very noisy environments.Cardioid&nbsp;&nbsp; also captures sound from the front but&nbsp; is better suited for quiet environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'such as podcasts or ASMR videos. Essentially,&nbsp; this simulates the function of a directional&nbsp;&nbsp; microphone. Figure-8 mode captures sound from&nbsp; the front and back, reducing side noise. This&nbsp;&nbsp; is well-suited for interviews or musical&nbsp; recordings involving multiple instruments.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:54] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing the "Voice Tone Preset" menu with options for "Standard," "Rich," and "Bright."
AUDIO: 'If you’re unsure which setting to use at&nbsp; first, leave the setting on “Omnidirectional.” And there’s another feature you can adjust&nbsp; anywhere that’s also very important: the voice&nbsp;&nbsp; tone preset. You can choose between Standard,&nbsp; Rich, and Bright. Let’s hear how they sound:&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Standard is designed for normal, everyday&nbsp; recording and works well in most situations;&nbsp;&nbsp; Full emphasizes the bass, giving you richer&nbsp; lows for a slightly more powerful sound;&nbsp;&nbsp; and Bright ensures exceptional voice clarity,&nbsp; making it ideal for teaching or streaming. As&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I mentioned, the voice tone preset can also&nbsp; be adjusted here on the camera, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp; or in the transmitter menu of the receiver. It’s&nbsp; time for us to take a closer look at the receiver. If you want to use the Mic Pro with a PC, a phone,&nbsp; or a larger camera, you should use the receiver.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The receiver has a dial on the front right for&nbsp; easier operation—you can also press it to confirm&nbsp;&nbsp; a selection, and pressing it twice exits the&nbsp; current menu. On the right side, the receiver&nbsp;&nbsp; has a power button to turn it on and off or to&nbsp; lock the screen. If you press the power button&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three times, the receiver enters pairing mode.&nbsp; This is necessary if you want to connect multiple&nbsp;&nbsp; receivers to the same transmitters; this is called&nbsp; “multi-receiver mode.” To connect the receiver to&nbsp;&nbsp; a smartphone or computer via USB-C, remove the&nbsp; cover on the back. Now you can insert the USB-C&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adapter here and connect the receiver via USB-C. You can also connect the receiver directly to a&nbsp;&nbsp; PC using a cable and without an adapter via&nbsp; the USB-C port on the back—and, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can use the port to charge it if you don’t&nbsp; have a charging case. Here on the left side,&nbsp;&nbsp; you’ll find a headphone jack. So you can&nbsp; plug in headphones and check whether the&nbsp;&nbsp; microphone volume is set correctly. And very&nbsp; importantly: there’s also an audio output here&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that lets you connect the receiver to a camera&nbsp; using an audio cable. It’s very easy to do.&nbsp; Attach the receiver to your camera’s cold&nbsp; shoe using the back clip, and use the audio&nbsp;&nbsp; cable to connect the receiver to the camera’s&nbsp; microphone input. The transmitter can also be&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'directly installed above the receiver and can be&nbsp; used as a directional microphone, for example, for&nbsp;&nbsp; vlogs or documentaries. However, the receiver’s&nbsp; back clip isn’t just for attaching it to a cold&nbsp;&nbsp; shoe—you can also use it to attach the receiver&nbsp; to your clothing or other objects, for example.'

[14:05] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing "RX Settings" with an icon for a microphone and a toggle switch.
AUDIO: 'Okay—let’s take a look at how to operate&nbsp; the receiver. And you’ll see—it has a lot&nbsp;&nbsp; of useful features. As I mentioned earlier,&nbsp; most of these features can also be activated&nbsp;&nbsp; in the Insta360 app or directly on the camera.&nbsp; But I’ll explain them here using the receiver.&nbsp;'

[14:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As soon as a microphone is connected, you\'ll see&nbsp; the input level here. In my case, it\'s Microphone&nbsp;&nbsp; 1—the connection quality is good—and the&nbsp; battery is fully charged. The receiver&nbsp;&nbsp; is set to mono—that\'s what the “M” stands for. You have two ways to operate the receiver—either&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'via the touchscreen or with the dial.&nbsp; When you turn the dial left or right,&nbsp;&nbsp; you select a menu item. And as mentioned, you&nbsp; can also press the dial to confirm a selection,&nbsp;&nbsp; for example, or press it twice to exit a menu.&nbsp; If you’re on the home screen and press the dial,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can use it to adjust the receiver’s gain—that&nbsp; is, to turn the volume of all sources up or down.&nbsp;&nbsp; Press it again to adjust the gain of the&nbsp; first microphone. Press it once more to&nbsp;&nbsp; adjust the input level of the second microphone. Your goal should always be to keep the level bar&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from turning yellow or red—otherwise, the audio&nbsp; recording will be distorted. If you tap the&nbsp;&nbsp; microphone’s level bar or status indicator, you&nbsp; can control a number of very important microphone&nbsp;&nbsp; functions. The field on the left lets you start&nbsp; and stop a remote internal recording; the one&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the middle lets you mute the microphone;&nbsp; and the one on the right lets you activate&nbsp;&nbsp; noise cancellation. This is one of the Mic Pr’s&nbsp; biggest strengths. There are two settings here:&nbsp;&nbsp; “weak” and “strong.” “Basic” is intended&nbsp; more for indoor use or mild background noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'while “Strong” ensures that your voice remains&nbsp; clear even in a noisy environment. And I&nbsp;&nbsp; have to say, it works exceptionally well. Now, you might have multiple transmitters&nbsp;&nbsp; connected and want to activate the same function&nbsp; for all of them at once. To do this, simply swipe&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up from the bottom edge. Now you can start an&nbsp; internal recording or activate noise cancellation&nbsp;&nbsp; for all microphones simultaneously. You can&nbsp; exit the menu by swiping down from the top.&nbsp; To open the receiver\'s main menu,&nbsp; swipe down from the top edge. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can change the receiver\'s settings. By default, even multiple transmitters&nbsp;&nbsp; together record a single mono track. If you’re&nbsp; recording with two transmitters, you can use the&nbsp;&nbsp; Stereo setting to split the signal into right and&nbsp; left channels. This can be quite useful and gives&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:15] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing the "Sync Camera Power" setting with "OFF" and "ON" options.
AUDIO: 'you more control in post-production, since you&nbsp; can then edit the channels separately—perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp; one voice is too quiet during an interview. Quadrophonic Sound even allows you to connect&nbsp;&nbsp; 4 transmitters and record 4 separate channels.&nbsp; However, this only works with very specific&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'devices. With “Safety Track,” the right channel&nbsp; records at a reduced gain—this ensures you always&nbsp;&nbsp; have at least one channel that shouldn’t clip. Here you can adjust the receiver’s&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'output gain again. The next feature is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp; useful when using a camera. When “Sync Camera&nbsp; Power” is enabled, the transmitter turns on and&nbsp;&nbsp; off along with the camera if it’s connected&nbsp; to a camera, for example via an audio cable.&nbsp;&nbsp; This prevents you from forgetting to turn the&nbsp; receiver on or off. And “Auto Power OFF” causes&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the transmitter to automatically shut down after&nbsp; 15 minutes if it’s not connected to a device. And, of course, you can also adjust a number&nbsp; of settings for the transmitters. You can&nbsp;&nbsp; enable a low-cut filter to filter out distracting&nbsp; low-frequency background noise. You can adjust the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gain—that is, the signal input—separately for&nbsp; each transmitter. We’ve already seen that this&nbsp;&nbsp; can also be done easily from the home screen. And&nbsp; TX Auto Gain is a particularly important feature. It automatically adjusts the&nbsp; gain depending on the situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Automatic Gain Control is better&nbsp; suited for situations with rapid,&nbsp;&nbsp; significant fluctuations in volume—such as&nbsp; noisy environments like sporting events or&nbsp;&nbsp; trade shows—while Dynamic Gain Control enables&nbsp; automatic adjustment for quieter indoor settings,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'allowing two people to share one mic from up&nbsp; to 1 meter away with automatic audio balancing.&nbsp; Right next to that, you’ll find the&nbsp; settings for the Directional Pickup&nbsp;&nbsp; Mode and the Voice Tone Preset. We’ve&nbsp; already covered both of these features.&nbsp;'

[17:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '32-bit float allows the transmitter to record&nbsp; audio files independently in 32-bit float,&nbsp;&nbsp; which enables better correction of the audio&nbsp; files in post-production. So if clipping occurs&nbsp;&nbsp; or a recording is too quiet, you can correct&nbsp; this much more effectively with a 32-bit&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'float recording. However, you should be aware&nbsp; that the 32-bit float feature applies only to&nbsp;&nbsp; internal recordings made with the transmitter&nbsp; and not to recordings made with the camera.&nbsp; The same applies to stereo internal recording.&nbsp; Here, you can enable the internal recording to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:26] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing "Processed File" options: "Original" and "Processed."
AUDIO: 'be made in stereo across two channels rather&nbsp; than in mono. This is possible because the&nbsp;&nbsp; Mic Pro has more than one microphone. Auto internal recording can also be very&nbsp;&nbsp; useful. When enabled, the microphone starts&nbsp; an internal recording if the signal to the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera or receiver becomes weak or the battery&nbsp; level gets low. That way, you’ll at least still&nbsp;&nbsp; have the internal audio recording. With Loop Recording, you can record&nbsp;&nbsp; internally in a continuous loop, with older&nbsp; recordings being overwritten continuously.&nbsp;'

[18:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The Recording File Format is also an interesting&nbsp; and important option. If you select “processed”&nbsp;&nbsp; here, the internal recording will be saved with&nbsp; the enabled effects—such as noise cancellation,&nbsp;&nbsp; low-cut filter, auto gain, and so on. If&nbsp; not, the original, unprocessed audio file&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is saved without these effects, but you’ll&nbsp; have more flexibility for post-processing.&nbsp; In addition, you can format the transmitter&nbsp; here, adjust settings for vibration or the&nbsp;&nbsp; LED light, or enable an auto-power-off&nbsp; function, just like with the receiver.&nbsp;'

[19:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As we saw earlier, the receiver has a&nbsp; headphone output so you can check the&nbsp;&nbsp; volume properly. Under “Monitor Settings,”&nbsp; you can set the headphone volume or choose&nbsp;&nbsp; which transmitters you want to test. Under “Connection,” you’ll see the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The screen of the Insta360 Mic Pro receiver showing the "General" settings menu.
AUDIO: 'transmitters paired with the receiver. You&nbsp; can remove them or pair another transmitter&nbsp;&nbsp; with the receiver. A single receiver&nbsp; can connect to up to 4 transmitters.&nbsp; The timecode function is also very important to&nbsp; many users. Its primary purpose is to synchronize&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recordings across multiple devices. The&nbsp; receiver can receive a timecode from an&nbsp;&nbsp; external device or output a timecode itself.&nbsp; You can also connect the Insta360 app here and&nbsp;&nbsp; configure a few general settings, such&nbsp; as language or time, on the far right.'

[20:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And connecting to the app is very important.&nbsp; That’s because you can use the app to update&nbsp;&nbsp; both your receiver and the transmitters. That’s&nbsp; why you should actually connect the microphone&nbsp;&nbsp; to the app right at the beginning to perform a&nbsp; possible firmware update. There are several ways&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to connect. Either select “Connect to App” here in&nbsp; the menu and then establish a wireless connection,&nbsp;&nbsp; or connect the receiver to your smartphone&nbsp; using a USB-C adapter and then launch the&nbsp;&nbsp; Insta360 app. As we’ve already seen, you&nbsp; can also connect the transmitters to the app&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:34] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A hand placing an Insta360 Mic Pro transmitter into its charging case.
AUDIO: 'individually. If an update is available, it will&nbsp; be displayed here immediately after you connect,&nbsp;&nbsp; and you should install it right away. If you&nbsp; have one receiver and multiple transmitters,&nbsp;&nbsp; you don’t need to update all devices individually.&nbsp; Connect the receiver to each transmitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then connect the receiver to your&nbsp; smartphone. As soon as you launch the app,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can update all devices in a single operation. Once you’re done recording, you can put everything&nbsp;&nbsp; back in the charging case. These two compartments&nbsp; are meant for the adapters—the USB-C adapter and,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if needed, the Lightning adapter. To keep from&nbsp; losing the two magnets, you can store them here&nbsp;&nbsp; on the sides at the back. You can store the&nbsp; back clips here on the back. The transmitters&nbsp;&nbsp; go in here on the sides. And the receiver,&nbsp; of course, goes in the middle. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you’ll always have to remove the windscreen.&nbsp; Otherwise, the charging case won’t close. You&nbsp;&nbsp; can charge the case or connect it to a computer&nbsp; via the USB-C port on the back. When the case is&nbsp;&nbsp; fully charged, all 4 LEDs on the side will light&nbsp; up. This allows for up to 30 hours of recording.'

[21:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now you know everything you need to&nbsp; successfully record for a long time&nbsp;&nbsp; with the mic pro. Give me a like as feedback&nbsp; if you found this video interesting. There&nbsp;&nbsp; will be more videos about the mic pro.&nbsp; So stay tuned, and see you next time.'
